@startuml 02-data-model
' =====================================================================
' Automated Code Feedback -- Logical Data Model
' Traces to: FR-6, FR-12..FR-18, FR-25, FR-26, FR-28, FR-30, NFR-6
'
' Changes in this revision:
'   assignment.grading_mode -- IMMEDIATE or NIGHTLY, per assignment.
'   submission_target.ai_needed -- defaults TRUE for every registered target
'     on submission. The instructor's test suite opts targets out during
'     functional testing. There is no resolution step.
'
' NOT stored here: student source files. Those stay on the container
' filesystem and are read from disk when building a prompt.
'
' Functional outcomes ARE stored, as test_result rows, but only because
' the instructor's suite reports them for the dashboard. We never derive
' them ourselves.
'
' results.json is never parsed to build a prompt. The prompt carries
' test_result.functional_test_feedback: whatever diagnostic her suite
' report. test_result.status is stored for the dashboard and is the only
' functional pass/fail record we hold. The AI phase reads results.json
' only to splice feedback into it.
' =====================================================================

title Logical Data Model

hide circle
' Do NOT add "hide methods" here. PlantUML treats any member containing
' parentheses as a method, which would hide the constraint lines.
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam linetype ortho

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
' Instructor configuration
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
entity "course" as course #DCEFD9 {
  * id : uuid <<PK>>
  --
  name : text
  term : text
}

entity "assignment" as assignment #DCEFD9 {
  * id : uuid <<PK>>
  --
  * course_id : uuid <<FK>>
  * platform : text <<GRADESCOPE>>
  * platform_assignment_id : text
  * grading_mode : text <<IMMEDIATE|NIGHTLY>>
  * language : text <<JAVA|PYTHON|...>>
  name : text
  is_active : boolean
  created_at : timestamptz
  --
  unique: platform, platform_assignment_id
  --
  language is what makes the source
  file derivable from a target's
  identifier, see AD-12. It cannot be
  inferred reliably: "foo.bar" is
  ambiguous between a Python module
  path and a Java package.
  --
  grading_mode decides WHEN AI grading
  happens, for the whole assignment:
    IMMEDIATE -- the AI phase grades
      during the submission run.
    NIGHTLY -- grading is left for a
      triggered rerun.
  --
  It lives here, not on the target, so
  a submission is wholly immediate or
  wholly nightly. Changing it needs no
  re-upload of the autograder zip.
}

entity "grading_target" as target #DCEFD9 {
  * id : uuid <<PK>>
  --
  * assignment_id : uuid <<FK>>
  * identifier : text
  * kind : text <<METHOD|CLASS|FILE>>
  max_score : numeric
  display_order : integer
  --
  FR-2, FR-3.
  --
  identifier plus kind plus the
  assignment's language determine which
  source file the AI phase reads. There
  is no file-pattern table: see AD-12.
  --
  No feedback_mode column. A target the
  instructor never wants AI feedback on
  simply is not registered, so a NEVER
  value would have nothing to describe.
  Whether a given SUBMISSION gets
  feedback on this target is
  submission_target.ai_needed.
}

entity "rubric" as rubric #DCEFD9 {
  * id : uuid <<PK>>
  --
  * grading_target_id : uuid <<FK>>
  * version : integer
  criteria_text : text
  feedback_guidance : text
  is_current : boolean
  created_at : timestamptz
  --
  FR-4, FR-5, FR-13.
  Versioned so feedback stays
  explainable after a rubric edit.
}

entity "llm_provider_config" as pcfg #DCEFD9 {
  * id : uuid <<PK>>
  --
  * provider : text <<ANTHROPIC|OPENAI|GOOGLE>>
  * model_name : text
  is_enabled : boolean
  is_primary : boolean
  --
  FR-23, NFR-11.
  Which providers and models to use,
  and which is primary. Never a key:
  per AD-4 the instructor's LLM API
  key lives in the autograder
  container's environment, so this
  table says WHAT to call and the
  container supplies the credential.
  --
  Returned to the AI phase as part of
  the AD-11 action response, since that
  is already the one call the container
  makes before doing any work.
}

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
' Runtime: written by the autograder script
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
entity "submission" as sub #FBE6D4 {
  * id : uuid <<PK>>
  --
  * assignment_id : uuid <<FK>>
  * platform_submission_id : text
  student_identifier : text
  * submitted_at : timestamptz
  ai_authorized_at : timestamptz
  * ai_graded_submitted_at : timestamptz
  is_active_attempt : boolean
  --
  unique: assignment_id,
          platform_submission_id
  --
  FR-18, FR-29, FR-30, NFR-6.
  See the guard note below.
  --
  is_active_attempt is UNUSED as drawn.
  It exists only in case OQ-4 is settled
  as "grade the latest attempt only".
  If OQ-4 lands the other way, delete
  it rather than leaving a column
  nothing reads.
}



entity "submission_target" as subtgt #FBE6D4 {
  * id : uuid <<PK>>
  --
  * submission_id : uuid <<FK>>
  * grading_target_id : uuid <<FK>>
  * ai_needed : boolean <<default TRUE>>
  completed_at : timestamptz
  last_error : text
  --
  unique: submission_id,
          grading_target_id
  --
  Created with ai_needed = TRUE for
  every registered target when the
  submission first reports in. The
  instructor's suite calls
  ai_not_needed on a target to switch
  it off, typically because its
  test passed and there is nothing left
  to explain.
  --
  No ai_needed_reason column. With one
  mechanism and a default of TRUE,
  "false" can only mean the suite opted
  out, so a reason would carry no
  information.
  --
  No ai_status enum either. Two columns
  say everything:
    completed_at NULL
      -> not attempted yet
    completed_at set, last_error NULL
      -> feedback produced
    completed_at set, last_error set
      -> attempted and failed; the
         message went to results.json
  --
  completed_at means "the AI phase
  finished with this row", not "it
  succeeded". Both outcomes are
  terminal, because there is no
  cross-night retry: a failure gets the
  same message in both grading modes.
  --
  No attempt_count either. Retries
  happen inside a single AI phase run
  and never outlive the process, so
  there is nothing to persist.
}

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
' AI-generated feedback
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
entity "test_result" as tres #FBE6D4 {
  * id : uuid <<PK>>
  --
  * submission_target_id : uuid <<FK>>
  * status : text <<PASS|FAIL|ERROR|SKIP>>
  functional_test_feedback : text
  recorded_at : timestamptz
  --
  OPTIONAL. Written during functional
  testing when the instructor's suite
  calls add_test_result on a target.
  --
  status is what the DASHBOARD needs.
  It is the only record of functional
  pass/fail that we hold.
  functional_test_feedback is what the
  PROMPT needs: the failing input,
  expected versus actual, whichever
  branch ran. It is what her test
  already worked out and would
  otherwise throw away.
  Two needs, one call, because she
  knows both at the same moment.
  --
  A child table rather than a column
  because a target usually has several
  tests. Rows are rendered into the
  prompt in recorded_at order.
  --
  No test_name column. Nothing would
  join on it or validate it, and she
  can name the test inside the note if
  it helps the model.
  --
  **Her report, not our parse.**
  results.json remains the
  student-facing record and is still
  never read to build a prompt. See
  AD-6.
  --
  Cap its length per row and per
  target: it is billed as input tokens
  on every provider call for that
  target.
}

entity "ai_feedback" as aifb #D6E4F0 {
  * id : uuid <<PK>>
  --
  * submission_target_id : uuid <<FK>>
  * rubric_id : uuid <<FK>>
  * provider : text
  * model_name : text
  prompt_version : text
  body : text
  suggested_score : numeric
  confidence : numeric
  is_selected : boolean
  input_tokens : integer
  output_tokens : integer
  generated_at : timestamptz
  --
  FR-15, FR-16, FR-25, FR-26.
  One row PER PROVIDER so FR-27
  comparison is a query, not a re-run.
  is_selected marks the row written
  into results.json.
  --
  Named ai_feedback, not feedback, to
  contrast with
  test_result.functional_test_feedback.
  Two unqualified senses of "feedback"
  in one schema is worse than a longer
  name.
  --
  The body column is not called
  feedback_text: inside a table already
  named ai_feedback that would stutter.
}

entity "ai_feedback_comparison" as aicmp #D6E4F0 {
  * id : uuid <<PK>>
  --
  * submission_target_id : uuid <<FK>>
  agreement_score : numeric
  score_spread : numeric
  selection_reason : text
  compared_at : timestamptz
  --
  FR-27, FR-28, NFR-12
}

entity "ai_feedback_review" as aireview #DCEFD9 {
  * id : uuid <<PK>>
  --
  * ai_feedback_id : uuid <<FK>>
  reviewer : text
  instructor_rating : integer
  instructor_comment : text
  reviewed_at : timestamptz
  --
  FR-11
}

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
' Audit
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
entity "trigger_run" as run #EDE0F0 {
  * id : uuid <<PK>>
  --
  * trigger : text <<SCHEDULE|MANUAL>>
  started_at : timestamptz
  finished_at : timestamptz
  status : text
  submissions_authorised : integer
  submissions_rerun : integer
  submissions_failed_to_trigger : integer
  log_uri : text
  --
  CI-3. Records which submissions the
  job clicked rerun on, not what was
  graded. Grading is logged per
  submission_target.
}

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
' Relationships
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
course ||--o{ assignment
assignment ||--o{ target
assignment ||--o{ sub
target ||--o{ rubric
target ||--o{ subtgt
sub ||--o{ subtgt
subtgt ||--o{ tres
subtgt ||--o{ aifb
subtgt ||--o| aicmp
rubric ||--o{ aifb
aifb ||--o{ aireview

note as N1 #FFF8DC
  **ai_graded_submitted_at: the token guard**
  A rerun re-executes the container against the
  same submitted files, so submitted_at does not
  change on rerun. That makes it a reliable
  marker of exactly which submission was graded.

  First thing the autograder does, before any
  task query and before any LLM call:

    if ai_graded_submitted_at IS NOT NULL
       AND ai_graded_submitted_at = submitted_at:
       skip AI grading, re-emit stored feedback

  Set it once every ai_needed row for the
  submission has completed_at set, whether that
  row succeeded or failed. Setting it on
  partial success would strand the failed
  targets permanently ungraded.

  A new student attempt gets a new
  platform_submission_id and its own row, so it
  is graded normally. The guard suppresses only
  re-grading of an already-graded attempt.

  Once set, it is never cleared. An AI-graded
  submission is finished: the nightly trigger
  stops selecting it, and the autograder skips
  the AI component if invoked for any other
  reason.
end note

note as N_AUTH #FFF8DC
  **ai_authorized_at: how the container tells
  Phase 1 from Phase 3.**
  In NIGHTLY mode the same autograder script runs
  twice, and the database looks identical both
  times: rows with ai_needed set and completed_at
  NULL. The container has no local way to know
  which run it is in, and the trigger cannot pass
  it anything, since all the trigger does is
  click a button.

  So the trigger records authorization BEFORE
  clicking rerun. Phase 1 finds it NULL and
  defers; Phase 3 finds it set and grades.

  Order matters: set it before the click, or the
  container can start and ask before the flag
  lands.

  Idempotent by design. If the flag is set but
  the rerun never happens, tomorrow's trigger
  simply tries again. The dangerous case is the
  trigger failing to set it at all, which looks
  exactly like "no feedback needed" -- so it
  belongs in the same trigger_run reporting as a
  failed click.
  --
  IMMEDIATE never uses this column. The decision
  is derived server-side, so the container does
  not know the rule.
end note

note as N1b #FFF8DC
  **How ai_needed gets its value**
  1. The submission first reports in. One
     submission_target row per registered target,
     ai_needed = TRUE.
  2. During functional testing the instructor's
     suite calls ai_not_needed(target) for the
     targets that need no commentary, usually the
     ones whose tests passed.
  3. The AI phase grades whatever is still TRUE.

  Any rule the instructor wants is expressible in
  step 2, because it is her own code: failures
  only, a subset, a partial-credit threshold, a
  cross-target condition. That is why there is no
  policy enum anywhere in this schema.

  The AI phase must also upsert these rows, for
  the case where the suite opts nothing out and
  therefore never calls us at all.
end note

note as N_CTX #FFF8DC
  **functional_test_feedback is untrusted input
  to a prompt.**
  Assertion messages routinely embed the
  student's own output, and sometimes their
  source. A student can therefore influence what
  lands in this column, which makes it a prompt
  injection surface: text that reads as an
  instruction rather than as data.

  Mitigations, none of them expensive:
  delimit it clearly in the prompt and label it
  as test output, keep the rubric authoritative
  over anything it says, cap its length, and do
  not let it carry the role of the guidance.

  The instructor writes the CALL, but she does
  not control the whole STRING. Those are
  different things and the design should assume
  the weaker one.
end note

note as N2 #FFF8DC
  **How many times results.json is written**
  Determined by the assignment's grading_mode,
  not by anything per target:
    IMMEDIATE -> once, during the submission
      run. The guard is set and the submission
      never enters the nightly queue.
    NIGHTLY -> twice. Once on the submission
      run carrying test results only, once on
      the triggered rerun carrying feedback.

  In NIGHTLY mode the first write has no AI
  feedback at all, which reads correctly as
  "not graded yet". A one-line "feedback arrives
  by tomorrow morning" marker per pending task
  is a courtesy worth adding, but nothing
  depends on it.
end note

note as N2b #FFF8DC
  **An unregistered target name is an error.**
  If the instructor's suite calls ai_not_needed
  with a name not registered on this assignment,
  the API rejects it with a 4xx naming it and
  listing the registered ones, and the library
  raises so her test run fails.

  This is now the only identifier check that can
  exist. Since ai_needed defaults to TRUE,
  there is no longer a "registered but never
  reported" case to detect: a target the suite
  never mentions simply keeps its default and
  gets graded.

  A narrower case survives: a target she renamed
  in the web app but never opts out will not
  trigger this check at all, since no call
  mentions it. The consequence is mild, though:
  it simply gets graded, and prompts do not
  depend on matching anything in results.json.
end note

note as N2c #FFF8DC
  **Open decisions still outstanding**
  OQ-1  "deduction" (FR-10) undefined. Derive as
        a view, not a column:
        feedback.suggested_score < max_score,
        since test outcomes are no longer in
        the database. See the dashboard note.
  OQ-2  confidence (FR-28) has a column but no
        agreed semantics.
  OQ-4  resubmissions. As drawn every attempt is
        graded. is_active_attempt exists if a
        latest-only policy is chosen.
end note

note as N3 #FFF8DC
  **Student code and FERPA**
  Student source code is no longer stored here:
  it stays on the autograder container and is
  read from disk when building a prompt. What
  this schema does hold is student identifiers
  and the AI feedback written about their work.

  In IMMEDIATE mode their code still reaches a
  third-party LLM within seconds of submission,
  it just is not retained by us. Privacy,
  retention and consent remain out of scope in
  the SRS, which is fine for a design document
  and not for a live course.
end note

note as N4 #FFF8DC
  **The dashboard reads test_result.status.**
  It is the only functional pass/fail record the
  system holds, and it exists because the
  instructor's suite reports it rather than
  because we parse anything.

  Coverage therefore depends on her calling
  add_test_result. The call is optional, so a
  suite that never uses it leaves the dashboard's
  pass/fail column empty. Worth stating in the
  instructor guide, so an empty column does not
  look like a bug.
end note

@enduml
